From ded4174479e3ee08c8d367fbd786fe4a569ca954 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Romain Dolbeau <romain@dolbeau.org>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 10:27:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] hw/scsi/ncr5380-isa: choose between the two chips

The card grows core=auto|sw|rtl.  With sw it embeds the software 5380
and its disks are QEMU's own, from -device scsi-hd; with rtl it loads
the Verilated wish5380 exactly as before.  auto keeps every existing
command line working: rtl if rtl= was given, sw otherwise.

A driver cannot tell the two apart, and that is the experiment.  The
same eight registers, two independent implementations - one derived from
the datasheet in SystemVerilog and one derived from it again in C - with
Linux's g_NCR5380 as the judge.

The contradictory combinations are refused by name rather than ignored:
core=rtl without rtl=, and core=sw with rtl= or image=, because on the
software path there is no SD card and a disk named that way would come
from somewhere the user did not mean.

All the pacing is now on the rtl path only.  It exists because a
Verilated core advances only when it is stepped and the target and SD
card behind it must advance while the guest is between accesses; the
software chip has no time in it, so the microsecond per access and the
500 us timer are simply not created.  On this card that reduces each
access handler to one call.

The software chip is built in realize rather than instance_init because
on the rtl path there is no software chip at all, and QEMU asserts that
every device it can see has been realized.

Its SCSI bus is initialised with the *board* as parent, which is what
esp.c does and not an accident: a device realized with no parent bus is
not reachable from the qdev tree, and neither is a bus hanging off one,
so -device scsi-hd would answer "No 'SCSI' bus found" and be right.
